// Formative practice quiz for updating-state-immutably. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/components-and-state/updating-state-immutably

::updating-state-immutably o1 - shallow spread::You write a copy of user using spread and override only name. user also has a nested address object. What is true of the copy?{
=It shares the same address object as the original#Correct. Spread is shallow, so nested objects are shared.
~It has its own deep copy of address#Not quite. Spread copies one level only.
~It has no address#Not quite. Spread copies all top-level properties, including address.
~It freezes the original object#Not quite. Spread does not freeze anything.
}

::updating-state-immutably o1 - nested update::To change person.pet.age in state without mutating anything, which objects need to be new?{
=Both person and pet#Correct. Copy at every level from the changed value up to the top.
~Only pet#Not quite. The new pet must go into a new person, or the state object is mutated.
~Only person#Not quite. That would either mutate pet or lose the new age.
~Neither, since assigning age directly is fine#Not quite. Direct assignment mutates state.
}

::updating-state-immutably o2 - remove from array::Which expression produces a new array without the item whose id matches?{
=filter, keeping items whose id differs#Correct. filter returns a new array of the items that pass.
~splice at the index of the item#Not quite. splice changes the original array.
~pop#Not quite. pop removes the last item from the original array.
~delete on the array element#Not quite. delete changes the original and leaves a hole.
}

::updating-state-immutably o2 - replace in array::You need to mark one item done in a state array. What is the right approach?{
=map over the array, and return a copy with done set for the matching item#Correct. A new array, and a new object only for the changed item.
~find the item and set its done property#Not quite. That mutates the item that is already in state.
~map over the array and set done on the matching item in place#Not quite. The array is new, but the item object has been mutated.
~sort the array first#Not quite. Sorting is unrelated, and in-place sort is itself a mutation.
}

::updating-state-immutably o3 - screen stays stale::A handler runs items.push, then setItems with items, and the screen does not change. Why?{
=The setter got the same array, so React saw no change#Correct. React compares with Object.is, which sees the same object.
~push does not add anything#Not quite. The item was added. The array identity did not change.
~Arrays are not allowed in state#Not quite. Arrays work fine in state when replaced with new ones.
~setItems must be awaited#Not quite. State setters do not return promises to await.
}
